284:
(a) Youth council members shall be selected through an administrative application and selection process established by a sub-selection committee of the youth activities committee.
(b) The sub-selection committee may review applications, interview candidates, evaluate qualifications, and select the youth council membership for every term.
(c) To be eligible, a youth council member shall generally:
(1) Be enrolled in high school;
(2) Reside within the city, or attend a school serving youth residents of the city, as permitted by city policy;
(3) Demonstrate an interest in leadership, civic engagement, government, and community service;
(4) Be in good academic and disciplinary standing, as determined through the city's application process; and
(5) Have parental or guardian consent where required.
(d) The youth activities committee may seek balanced representation from public, private, charter, home school, or other eligible student populations as determined appropriate.
(Ord. No. 1257, § 1, 5-21-2026)

285:
(a) The youth council shall solicit, consider, and communicate the ideas, concerns, and perspectives of the city's youth on matters affecting youth and the broader community.
(b) The youth council shall serve in an advisory capacity only and shall have no binding authority over city staff, the youth activities committee, or city council for any recommendation, proposal, or vote taken.
(c) The duties of the youth council may include, but are not limited to:
(1) Serving as a forum to help identify the needs and concerns of youth within the city;
(2) Learning about the operations, responsibilities, and processes of local government;
(3) Advising the youth activities committee and city staff on matters affecting youth;
(4) Participating in youth leadership development, service projects, civic engagement programs, workshops, educational opportunities, conferences, and special events;
(5) Assisting with the planning, sponsorship, and coordination of youth-related programs, activities, and events;
(6) Participating in city-approved trips, site visits, and educational experiences intended to strengthen civic knowledge, leadership skills, or understanding of government and community institutions;
(7) Promoting communication between the city and its youth residents; and
(8) Encouraging positive citizenship, volunteerism, leadership, and community involvement among youth.
(Ord. No. 1257, § 1, 5-21-2026)

286:
The youth council may make recommendations to the youth activities committee, city staff, and the mayor and council, either directly or through the youth activities committee or designee, regarding youth-related projects, activities, concerns, opportunities, educational experiences, and leadership initiatives, including, but not limited to:
(1) Recommending ways to increase youth participation in city government and community life;
(2) Recommending youth service projects, recognition programs, educational experiences, or outreach efforts;
(3) Recommending strategies to improve civic awareness among youth;
(4) Providing feedback on city programs, facilities, or initiatives affecting youth;
(5) Assisting in identifying emerging youth-related issues or opportunities; and
(6) Recommending projects or activities that benefit the city and its youth population.
(Ord. No. 1257, § 1, 5-21-2026)

292:
(a) The youth activities committee shall solicit comments, concerns, and advice from city residents, students, parents, schools, civic organizations, and city staff regarding youth leadership development, civic education, youth programming, service opportunities, youth travel and educational opportunities, and youth-related community needs.
(b) The committee shall evaluate such comments, concerns, and proposals and may develop recommendations prioritized according to feasibility, public benefit, staffing capacity, safety, supervision requirements, funding parameters, and consistency with city goals.
(c) The committee shall serve in an advisory capacity only and shall have no binding authority over city staff or city council for any recommendation, proposal, or vote taken.
(d) The duties of the committee may include, but are not limited to:
(1) Supporting the creation, operation, recruitment, and continuity of a city youth council;
(2) Reviewing and recommending criteria, procedures, timelines, and standards for youth council applications, administrative selection, orientation, and participation;
(3) Establishing from among its members a sub-selection committee to review youth council applications, interviewing applicants if desired, and selecting members for the youth council every term;
(4) Encouraging collaboration among the city, schools, nonprofit organizations, civic groups, businesses, and the community with the youth council in matters affecting youth;
(5) Recommending youth-focused projects, events, programs, educational experiences, conferences, site visits, leadership workshops, and trips that employ the time and talents of the youth council in a wholesome and constructive manner;
(6) Assisting city staff in identifying youth-related needs, trends, and opportunities within the city;
(7) Promoting youth involvement in local government and community life; and
(8) Recommending strategies to strengthen communication between the city and youth residents.
(Ord. No. 1258, § 1, 5-21-2026)

293:
The committee may make recommendations to the mayor and council and city staff for new or revised youth-related projects, programs, policies, initiatives, educational opportunities, and trips referred to it by the city manager, city staff, or mayor and council, including, but not limited to:
(1) Recommending measures to improve youth engagement in civic affairs;
(2) Recommending youth leadership development and service programming;
(3) Recommending outreach and educational strategies related to the city's youth;
(4) Reviewing the status of youth council operations and youth programming annually;
(5) Collaborating with staff on annual priorities for youth-related and youth council initiatives;
(6) Identifying partnerships or sponsorship opportunities for youth council activities, travel, and educational experiences, subject to city approval; and
(7) Recommending methods to recognize and promote youth achievement, service, leadership, and civic participation.
(Ord. No. 1258, § 1, 5-21-2026)

